{
  "_comment": "Destination grant patterns as typed after `grant destination` (or written in the config, or bound as a channel), and whether Sentrook takes them (`ok`). A wildcard that can reach a host or a mail domain may only be `*.` and one registrable domain: at least two labels, not a suffix many owners share (`com`, `co.uk`, `com.au`) and not part of an address written in numbers, read as a credential grant reads its host. A wildcard in a channel's name or id after a platform prefix or `#`, or in the name before an `@`, is fine. A pattern with nothing literal is refused too. The scanner (`prepare.grants.destination_ok`: such a grant is reported as lapsed, `too_broad`, and covers nothing) and both plugins (the grant command refuses it with `refusal`, {pattern} replaced by the pattern as typed; a config grant with it is never sent) must agree on every case. `target`: a host, mail address (with `@`) or channel (with a platform prefix or `#`) the pattern's glob reaches; the scanner covers it only when the pattern is `ok`.",
  "refusal": "\"{pattern}\" is too broad for a destination grant: a wildcard may cover only one registrable domain (*.example.com), a channel's name (discord:#ops-*) or the name before an @ (*@example.com), never a suffix many owners share, such as *.com or *.co.uk.",
